// Auto-extracted static data for Maths Study Hub. Loaded before the main script in index.html.
// Edit data here; app logic stays in index.html.
const GLOSSARY=[{"n":"0","topic":"Foundations: Number, Notation & Arithmetic","cluster":"foundations","items":[{"term":"Natural Numbers (ℕ)","def":"The counting numbers: 1, 2, 3, 4, … Some definitions include 0; at A Level, context will clarify. Denoted by the symbol ℕ.","eg":"3, 17, and 100 are all natural numbers.","sub":"Types of Numbers","note":"0 is sometimes included (ℕ₀) but this is not standard in UK A Level.","il":["Natural Numbers"]},{"term":"Integers (ℤ)","def":"All whole numbers, positive, negative, and zero: …, −3, −2, −1, 0, 1, 2, 3, … Denoted ℤ (from German Zahlen).","eg":"−5, 0, and 12 are integers. 2.5 is not.","sub":"Types of Numbers","il":["Integers"]},{"term":"Rational Numbers (ℚ)","def":"Numbers that can be expressed as a fraction p/q where p and q are integers and q ≠ 0. Their decimal expansions either terminate or recur.","eg":"3/4 = 0.75 (terminates); 1/3 = 0.333… (recurs). Both are rational.","sub":"Types of Numbers","note":"Every integer is rational (write n as n/1).","il":["Rational Numbers"]},{"term":"Irrational Numbers","def":"Numbers that cannot be expressed as a fraction of two integers. Their decimal expansions are non-terminating and non-repeating.","eg":"√2 ≈ 1.41421… and π ≈ 3.14159… are both irrational.","sub":"Types of Numbers","note":"The sum or product of two irrationals is not always irrational: √2 × √2 = 2.","il":["Irrational Numbers"]},{"term":"Real Numbers (ℝ)","def":"All rational and irrational numbers combined. Every point on the number line corresponds to a real number. Denoted ℝ.","eg":"−7, 0, 1/2, √5, and π are all real numbers.","sub":"Types of Numbers","note":"At A Level, unless stated otherwise, assume all numbers are real.","il":["Real Numbers"]},{"term":"Prime Number","def":"A natural number greater than 1 with exactly two distinct factors: 1 and itself.","eg":"2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13 are prime. 1 is NOT prime. 4 is not prime (factors: 1, 2, 4).","sub":"Types of Numbers","note":"2 is the only even prime number.","il":["Prime Number"]},{"term":"Composite Number","def":"A natural number greater than 1 that has at least one factor other than 1 and itself; i.e. not prime.","eg":"6 = 2 × 3 is composite.","sub":"Types of Numbers","il":["Composite Number"]},{"term":"Factor / Divisor","def":"An integer that divides another exactly with no remainder. These two words are interchangeable.","eg":"Factors of 12 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12.","sub":"Types of Numbers","il":["Divisor"]},{"term":"Multiple","def":"A number obtained by multiplying a given integer by any natural number.","eg":"Multiples of 5: 5, 10, 15, 20, …","sub":"Types of Numbers"},{"term":"HCF (Highest Common Factor)","def":"The largest positive integer that divides two or more numbers exactly. Also called GCD (Greatest Common Divisor) — these are fully interchangeable.","eg":"HCF(12, 18) = 6.","sub":"Types of Numbers","il":["Highest Common Factor","HCF"]},{"term":"LCM (Lowest Common Multiple)","def":"The smallest positive integer that is a multiple of two or more given numbers.","eg":"LCM(4, 6) = 12.","sub":"Types of Numbers","il":["Lowest Common Multiple","LCM"]},{"term":"Expression","def":"A mathematical phrase built from numbers, variables, and operations. Unlike an equation, it has no equals sign.","eg":"3x² + 2x − 5 is an expression.","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language"},{"term":"Equation","def":"A statement that two expressions are equal, containing an equals sign. Equations can be solved to find the value(s) of the unknown.","eg":"3x + 1 = 10 is an equation. Solving gives x = 3.","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language","note":"Contrast with an identity (true for all values) and an expression (no equals sign)."},{"term":"Identity","def":"A statement that is true for ALL values of the variable(s). Written with ≡ instead of =.","eg":"(x + 1)² ≡ x² + 2x + 1. This holds for every value of x.","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language","note":"An equation is only true for specific x values; an identity is always true."},{"term":"Formula","def":"An equation that expresses a relationship between quantities, usually with a specific purpose or derived result.","eg":"The quadratic formula x = (−b ± √(b²−4ac)) / 2a is a formula for solving quadratics.","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language"},{"term":"Variable","def":"A symbol (usually a letter) that represents an unknown or changing quantity.","eg":"In y = 2x + 3, both x and y are variables.","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language"},{"term":"Constant","def":"A fixed value that does not change. In a formula, constants are distinguished from variables.","eg":"In y = 3x + 7, the number 7 is a constant. The number e ≈ 2.718 is also a mathematical constant.","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language"},{"term":"Coefficient","def":"The numerical factor multiplying a variable or power of a variable in a term.","eg":"In 5x², the coefficient of x² is 5. In 3xy, the coefficient of xy is 3.","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language"},{"term":"Term","def":"A single component of an expression, separated from other terms by + or −. A term can be a number, a variable, or a product of numbers and variables.","eg":"In 4x² − 3x + 7, the three terms are 4x², −3x, and 7.","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language"},{"term":"Like Terms","def":"Terms that have the same variable(s) raised to the same power(s). Only like terms can be added or subtracted.","eg":"3x² and −7x² are like terms. 3x and 3x² are NOT like terms.","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language","il":["Like Terms"]},{"term":"Polynomial","def":"An expression consisting of a sum of terms, each being a non-negative integer power of a variable multiplied by a coefficient.","eg":"4x³ − 2x + 1 is a polynomial. 1/x is NOT (negative power).","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language","note":"Degree of a polynomial = highest power present.","il":["Polynomial"]},{"term":"Degree (of a polynomial)","def":"The highest power of the variable in the polynomial.","eg":"5x⁴ + 3x² − 1 has degree 4 (it is a quartic).","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language","il":["of a polynomial"]},{"term":"Linear","def":"A polynomial of degree 1. Graphically, a straight line.","eg":"2x + 5 is linear.","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language"},{"term":"Quadratic","def":"A polynomial of degree 2. Graphically, a parabola.","eg":"x² − 3x + 2 is quadratic.","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language","il":["Quadratic"]},{"term":"Cubic","def":"A polynomial of degree 3.","eg":"x³ + 2x² − x + 4 is cubic.","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language","il":["Cubic"]},{"term":"Quartic","def":"A polynomial of degree 4.","eg":"x⁴ − 5x² + 4 is quartic.","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language","il":["Quartic"]},{"term":"Root / Zero (of a polynomial or equation)","def":"A value of x for which the expression equals zero. These two terms are interchangeable. Also called a solution when in the context of an equation.","eg":"The roots of x² − 5x + 6 = 0 are x = 2 and x = 3.","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language","note":"'Root', 'zero', and 'solution' all mean the same thing in this context."},{"term":"Rational Expression","def":"A fraction where both numerator and denominator are polynomials.","eg":"(3x + 1) / (x² − 4) is a rational expression.","sub":"Basic Algebraic Language","il":["Rational Expression"]},{"term":"∈ (Element of)","def":"Means 'is a member / element of' a set.","eg":"3 ∈ ℤ reads '3 is an element of the integers.'","sub":"Notation & Symbols","il":["Element of"]},{"term":"∉ (Not element of)","def":"Means 'is NOT a member of' a set.","eg":"√2 ∉ ℚ reads '√2 is not rational.'","sub":"Notation & Symbols","il":["Not element of"]},{"term":"∀ (For all)","def":"A universal quantifier meaning 'for every' or 'for all'.","eg":"∀ x ∈ ℝ, x² ≥ 0 reads 'for all real x, x² is non-negative.'","sub":"Notation & Symbols","il":["For all"]},{"term":"∃ (There exists)","def":"An existential quantifier meaning 'there is at least one'.","eg":"∃ x ∈ ℝ such that x² = 4.","sub":"Notation & Symbols","il":["There exists"]},{"term":"⇒ (Implies)","def":"If statement A is true then statement B must also be true. A ⇒ B. Does NOT mean the converse is true.","eg":"x = 3 ⇒ x² = 9. (But x² = 9 does not necessarily mean x = 3.)","sub":"Notation & Symbols","il":["Implies"]},{"term":"⇐ (Implied by)","def":"B is implied by A; same as A ⇒ B written in reverse.","eg":"x² = 9 ⇐ x = 3.","sub":"Notation & Symbols","il":["Implied by"]},{"term":"⟺ (If and only if / iff)","def":"A is true exactly when B is true; implication goes both ways. Also written 'iff'.","eg":"x² = 4 ⟺ x = 2 or x = −2.","sub":"Notation & Symbols"},{"term":"|x| (Modulus / Absolute Value)","def":"The distance of x from zero on the number line; always non-negative. |x| = x if x ≥ 0, and |x| = −x if x < 0.","eg":"|−5| = 5, |3| = 3, |0| = 0.","sub":"Notation & Symbols","note":"Also called 'absolute value' — these terms are interchangeable."},{"term":"≈ (Approximately equal to)","def":"Used when a value is rounded or estimated.","eg":"π ≈ 3.14159.","sub":"Notation & Symbols","il":["Approximately equal to"]},{"term":"∝ (Proportional to)","def":"y ∝ x means y = kx for some constant k.","eg":"The circumference C of a circle is proportional to its diameter d: C ∝ d, so C = πd.","sub":"Notation & Symbols","il":["Proportional to"]},{"term":"Σ (Sigma — Summation)","def":"Denotes the sum of a sequence of terms. Σ from r=1 to n of f(r) means add up f(1) + f(2) + … + f(n).","eg":"Σ(r=1 to 4) r = 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10.","sub":"Notation & Symbols"},{"term":"n! (Factorial)","def":"The product of all positive integers up to n. n! = n × (n−1) × … × 2 × 1. By convention, 0! = 1.","eg":"5! = 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120.","sub":"Notation & Symbols","il":["Factorial"]},{"term":"ⁿCᵣ / C(n,r) / 'n choose r' (Binomial Coefficient)","def":"The number of ways of choosing r items from n without regard to order. ⁿCᵣ = n! / (r!(n−r)!). Also written as a vertical array in parentheses.","eg":"⁵C₂ = 5!/(2!3!) = 10.","sub":"Notation & Symbols","note":"This appears on your formula sheet.","il":["Binomial Coefficient","n choose r"]},{"term":"Index / Exponent / Power","def":"The small raised number indicating how many times the base is multiplied by itself. These three words are fully interchangeable.","eg":"In 2⁵, the index (exponent/power) is 5, and 2⁵ = 32.","sub":"Indices & Surds","il":["Exponent"]},{"term":"Base","def":"The number being raised to a power.","eg":"In 3⁴, the base is 3.","sub":"Indices & Surds"},{"term":"Index Law 1 — Multiplication: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ","def":"When multiplying two powers of the same base, add the indices.","eg":"x³ × x⁵ = x⁸.","sub":"Indices & Surds"},{"term":"Index Law 2 — Division: aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ","def":"When dividing two powers of the same base, subtract the indices.","eg":"x⁷ ÷ x² = x⁵.","sub":"Indices & Surds"},{"term":"Index Law 3 — Power of a power: (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ","def":"When raising a power to another power, multiply the indices.","eg":"(x²)⁴ = x⁸.","sub":"Indices & Surds"},{"term":"Zero Index: a⁰ = 1","def":"Any non-zero number raised to the power 0 equals 1.","eg":"7⁰ = 1, (−3)⁰ = 1.","sub":"Indices & Surds","note":"0⁰ is undefined."},{"term":"Negative Index: a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ","def":"A negative index means the reciprocal of the positive power.","eg":"x⁻³ = 1/x³. 2⁻¹ = 1/2.","sub":"Indices & Surds"},{"term":"Fractional Index: a^(1/n) = ⁿ√a and a^(m/n) = (ⁿ√a)ᵐ","def":"A fractional index represents a root. The denominator is the root, the numerator is the power.","eg":"8^(2/3) = (∛8)² = 2² = 4.","sub":"Indices & Surds"},{"term":"Surd","def":"An irrational root that cannot be simplified to a rational number. Left in root form for exactness.","eg":"√3, √7, ∛5 are surds. √4 = 2 is NOT a surd (it is rational).","sub":"Indices & Surds","il":["Surd"]},{"term":"Simplifying Surds","def":"Rewrite √(ab) = √a × √b to extract perfect square factors.","eg":"√48 = √(16 × 3) = 4√3.","sub":"Indices & Surds","il":["Simplifying Surds"]},{"term":"Rationalising the Denominator","def":"Removing a surd from the denominator of a fraction by multiplying numerator and denominator by a suitable expression. For 1/√a, multiply by √a/√a. For 1/(a+√b), use the conjugate (a−√b).","eg":"1/√3 = √3/3. And 1/(1+√2) × (1−√2)/(1−√2) = (1−√2)/(1−2) = √2−1.","sub":"Indices & Surds","il":["Rationalising the Denominator"]},{"term":"Conjugate (Surd)","def":"For an expression a + √b, its conjugate is a − √b. Their product (a+√b)(a−√b) = a²−b is rational.","eg":"Conjugate of 3 + √5 is 3 − √5. Their product = 9 − 5 = 4.","sub":"Indices & Surds","il":["Surd","Conjugate"]}]},{"n":"1","topic":"Algebra & Functions","cluster":"algebra","items":[{"term":"Quadratic Function","def":"A function of the form f(x) = ax² + bx + c where a ≠ 0. Its graph is a parabola.","eg":"f(x) = 2x² − 3x + 1.","sub":"Quadratics","il":["Quadratic Function"]},{"term":"Discriminant","def":"For ax² + bx + c = 0, the discriminant is Δ = b² − 4ac. It determines the nature of the roots: Δ > 0 → two distinct real roots; Δ = 0 → one repeated (equal) real root; Δ < 0 → no real roots (complex roots).","eg":"For x² − 5x + 6: Δ = 25 − 24 = 1 > 0, so two distinct real roots (x=2 and x=3).","sub":"Quadratics","il":["Discriminant"]},{"term":"Completing the Square","def":"Rewriting ax² + bx + c in the form a(x + p)² + q. Useful for finding the vertex, solving quadratics, and sketching parabolas.","eg":"x² + 6x + 5 = (x+3)² − 9 + 5 = (x+3)² − 4. Vertex at (−3, −4).","sub":"Quadratics","il":["Completing the Square"]},{"term":"Quadratic Formula","def":"For ax² + bx + c = 0: x = (−b ± √(b²−4ac)) / 2a. Derived by completing the square on the general form.","eg":"2x² − 3x − 2 = 0 → x = (3 ± √(9+16))/4 = (3 ± 5)/4 → x=2 or x=−1/2.","sub":"Quadratics","note":"This formula is given on the formula sheet.","il":["Quadratic Formula"]},{"term":"Factorising a Quadratic","def":"Expressing ax² + bx + c as a product of two linear factors. The most elegant method when integer roots exist.","eg":"x² − 5x + 6 = (x−2)(x−3).","sub":"Quadratics","il":["Factorising a Quadratic"]},{"term":"Vertex of a Parabola","def":"The turning point of a parabola — the minimum point if a > 0, maximum if a < 0. Found by completing the square: a(x+p)² + q has vertex (−p, q).","eg":"y = (x+3)² − 4 has vertex at (−3, −4).","sub":"Quadratics","il":["Vertex of a Parabola"]},{"term":"Repeated Root / Equal Roots","def":"When Δ = 0, the quadratic has one root counted twice. The parabola touches the x-axis at exactly one point.","eg":"x² − 4x + 4 = (x−2)² = 0 → x = 2 (repeated).","sub":"Quadratics","il":["Repeated Root","Equal Roots"]},{"term":"Simultaneous Equations","def":"A set of equations with the same variables that must all be satisfied at the same time.","eg":"Solve x + y = 5 and 2x − y = 1 simultaneously → x=2, y=3.","sub":"Simultaneous Equations","il":["Simultaneous Equations"]},{"term":"Elimination Method","def":"Add or subtract multiples of the equations to eliminate one variable.","eg":"Adding x+y=5 and 2x−y=1 gives 3x=6, so x=2.","sub":"Simultaneous Equations","il":["Elimination Method"]},{"term":"Substitution Method","def":"Rearrange one equation to express one variable in terms of the other, then substitute into the second equation.","eg":"From x+y=5, get y=5−x. Substitute into y=x²+1 to solve.","sub":"Simultaneous Equations","il":["Substitution Method"]},{"term":"Inequality","def":"A mathematical statement that one quantity is greater than, less than, greater than or equal to, or less than or equal to another. Symbols: >, <, ≥, ≤.","eg":"3x + 1 > 7 → x > 2.","sub":"Inequalities","note":"When multiplying or dividing both sides by a NEGATIVE number, flip the inequality sign.","il":["Inequality"]},{"term":"Strict Inequality","def":"An inequality using > or <, meaning the boundary value itself is excluded.","eg":"x > 3 excludes x = 3 itself.","sub":"Inequalities","il":["Strict Inequality"]},{"term":"Linear Inequality","def":"An inequality involving only linear (degree 1) terms.","eg":"2x − 3 < 5 → x < 4.","sub":"Inequalities","il":["Linear Inequality"]},{"term":"Quadratic Inequality","def":"An inequality involving a quadratic. Solve by first finding roots, then using a sketch or sign diagram to determine where the quadratic is positive or negative.","eg":"x² − x − 6 > 0 → (x−3)(x+2) > 0 → x < -2 or x > 3.","sub":"Inequalities","il":["Quadratic Inequality"]},{"term":"Set Notation for Inequalities","def":"{x : x > 3} means 'the set of all x such that x > 3'. Union ∪ means 'or'; intersection ∩ means 'and'.","eg":"{x: x<-1} ∪ {x: x>4} represents x<-1 or x>4.","sub":"Inequalities","il":["Set Notation for Inequalities"]},{"term":"Factor Theorem","def":"If f(a) = 0, then (x − a) is a factor of f(x). Conversely, if (x − a) is a factor, then f(a) = 0. Extended: if f(b/a) = 0, then (ax − b) is a factor.","eg":"f(x) = x³ − 7x + 6. f(1) = 1−7+6 = 0, so (x−1) is a factor.","sub":"Polynomials & Algebraic Division","il":["Factor Theorem"]},{"term":"Remainder Theorem","def":"When f(x) is divided by (x − a), the remainder is f(a).","eg":"Remainder when x³ + 2x − 1 is divided by (x−2) = f(2) = 8+4−1 = 11.","sub":"Polynomials & Algebraic Division","il":["Remainder Theorem"]},{"term":"Polynomial Long Division","def":"A method to divide one polynomial by another (of lower or equal degree), analogous to numerical long division.","eg":"Divide x³ − 2x² + 3x − 1 by (x−1): the quotient is x² − x + 2 remainder 1.","sub":"Polynomials & Algebraic Division","il":["Polynomial Long Division"]},{"term":"Algebraic Long Division","def":"Same as polynomial long division — these terms are interchangeable.","eg":"See Polynomial Long Division above.","sub":"Polynomials & Algebraic Division","il":["Algebraic Long Division"]},{"term":"Function","def":"A rule that assigns exactly one output to each input in its domain. Written f(x) or f: x ↦ f(x). Each input maps to exactly one output (one-to-one or many-to-one).","eg":"f(x) = x² is a function. Each x gives exactly one x².","sub":"Functions","note":"A one-to-many mapping is NOT a function (e.g. x ↦ ±√x).","il":["Function"]},{"term":"Domain","def":"The set of all permitted input values (x-values) for a function.","eg":"For f(x) = √x, the domain is x ≥ 0 (x ∈ ℝ, x ≥ 0), since you cannot take the square root of a negative number in ℝ.","sub":"Functions"},{"term":"Range / Codomain","def":"The set of all possible output values (y-values / f(x) values) produced by the function acting on its domain. 'Range' and 'image set' are interchangeable in A Level context.","eg":"For f(x) = x², domain ℝ, the range is f(x) ≥ 0.","sub":"Functions","note":"The range depends on the domain — always state them together.","il":["Codomain"]},{"term":"One-to-One Function","def":"Each input maps to a unique output AND each output comes from a unique input. Required for an inverse to exist.","eg":"f(x) = 2x + 1 is one-to-one.","sub":"Functions","note":"f(x) = x² is many-to-one (both 3 and −3 map to 9) and does NOT have an inverse over ℝ.","il":["One-to-One Function"]},{"term":"Many-to-One Function","def":"Multiple different inputs can produce the same output.","eg":"f(x) = x² maps both 2 and −2 to 4.","sub":"Functions","il":["Many-to-One Function"]},{"term":"Inverse Function (f⁻¹)","def":"The function that 'undoes' f. If f(a) = b, then f⁻¹(b) = a. The graph of y = f⁻¹(x) is the reflection of y = f(x) in the line y = x. Only one-to-one functions have inverses.","eg":"If f(x) = 2x+3, then f⁻¹(x) = (x−3)/2.","sub":"Functions","il":["Inverse Function"]},{"term":"Composite Function (fg or f∘g)","def":"Apply g first, then apply f to the result. fg(x) = f(g(x)).","eg":"If f(x) = x² and g(x) = x+1, then fg(x) = f(g(x)) = (x+1)².","sub":"Functions","note":"fg ≠ gf in general. Order matters.","il":["Composite Function"]},{"term":"Modulus Function / Absolute Value Function","def":"f(x) = |x|. Outputs the non-negative value of x. The graph has a V-shape. |x| = x for x≥0, |x| = −x for x<0.","eg":"y = |2x−3|: for x≥3/2, y=2x−3; for x<3/2, y=3−2x.","sub":"Functions","il":["Modulus Function","Absolute Value Function"]},{"term":"Translation","def":"Shifting the graph without changing its shape. y = f(x) + a shifts UP by a. y = f(x+a) shifts LEFT by a.","eg":"y = sin(x) + 2 shifts the sine curve up 2 units.","sub":"Graph Transformations","note":"Note: f(x+a) moves LEFT (counter-intuitive!). f(x−a) moves RIGHT.","il":["Translation"]},{"term":"Stretch (Vertical)","def":"y = af(x) stretches the graph by scale factor a in the y-direction (parallel to y-axis).","eg":"y = 2sin(x) doubles all y-values, stretching vertically by factor 2.","sub":"Graph Transformations","il":["Vertical","Stretch"]},{"term":"Stretch (Horizontal)","def":"y = f(ax) stretches the graph by scale factor 1/a in the x-direction (parallel to x-axis).","eg":"y = sin(2x) compresses the x-direction by factor 1/2 (period halved).","sub":"Graph Transformations","note":"A horizontal stretch by factor 1/a means the x-coordinates are multiplied by 1/a.","il":["Horizontal","Stretch"]},{"term":"Reflection in the x-axis","def":"y = −f(x) reflects the graph in the x-axis; all y-values are negated.","eg":"y = −x² reflects the parabola downward.","sub":"Graph Transformations","il":["Reflection in the x-axis"]},{"term":"Reflection in the y-axis","def":"y = f(−x) reflects the graph in the y-axis; all x-values are negated.","eg":"y = (−x)³ = −x³ reflects the cubic.","sub":"Graph Transformations","il":["Reflection in the y-axis"]},{"term":"Partial Fractions","def":"Decomposing a rational expression into a sum of simpler fractions. Used extensively in integration. For linear factors: A/(ax+b) + B/(cx+d). For repeated factors: A/(ax+b) + B/(ax+b)².","eg":"(5x+1)/((x+1)(x−2)) = A/(x+1) + B/(x−2). Solving: A=−4/3... (cover-up or equating coefficients).","sub":"Partial Fractions","il":["Partial Fractions"]},{"term":"Improper Fraction (Algebraic)","def":"A rational expression where the degree of the numerator is ≥ degree of the denominator. Must first perform polynomial division before splitting into partial fractions.","eg":"(x² + 3x + 1)/(x+1) → divide first to get x+2 with remainder −1, then handle the remainder.","sub":"Partial Fractions","il":["Algebraic","Improper Fraction"]}]},{"n":"2","topic":"Coordinate Geometry","cluster":"coord","items":[{"term":"Cartesian Coordinates","def":"A system for specifying points in a plane using an ordered pair (x, y) relative to two perpendicular axes.","eg":"The point (3, −2) is 3 units right and 2 units down from the origin.","sub":"","il":["Cartesian Coordinates"]},{"term":"Gradient (Slope)","def":"A measure of the steepness of a line. Gradient m = (y₂−y₁)/(x₂−x₁). Positive = upward, negative = downward, zero = horizontal.","eg":"Gradient of line through (1,2) and (3,8): m = (8−2)/(3−1) = 3.","sub":"","note":"'Gradient' and 'slope' are interchangeable.","il":["Slope"]},{"term":"y-intercept","def":"The y-coordinate where a graph crosses the y-axis (where x = 0).","eg":"y = 3x + 5 has y-intercept 5.","sub":"","il":["y-intercept"]},{"term":"x-intercept / Root","def":"The x-coordinate(s) where a graph crosses the x-axis (where y = 0).","eg":"y = 3x − 6 has x-intercept at x = 2.","sub":"","il":["x-intercept"]},{"term":"Equation of a Straight Line — y = mx + c","def":"Standard form. m is the gradient, c is the y-intercept.","eg":"Gradient 2, y-intercept −3: y = 2x − 3.","sub":""},{"term":"Equation of a Straight Line — y − y₁ = m(x − x₁)","def":"Point-slope form. Useful when you know the gradient and a point on the line.","eg":"Line through (2, 5) with gradient 3: y − 5 = 3(x − 2) → y = 3x − 1.","sub":""},{"term":"Parallel Lines","def":"Two lines are parallel if and only if they have the same gradient. They never intersect.","eg":"y = 2x + 1 and y = 2x − 4 are parallel (both gradient 2).","sub":"","il":["Parallel Lines"]},{"term":"Perpendicular Lines","def":"Two lines are perpendicular if the product of their gradients is −1: m₁ × m₂ = −1, so m₂ = −1/m₁.","eg":"If one line has gradient 3, a perpendicular line has gradient −1/3.","sub":"","il":["Perpendicular Lines"]},{"term":"Distance Between Two Points","def":"d = √((x₂−x₁)² + (y₂−y₁)²). This follows directly from Pythagoras' theorem.","eg":"Distance from (1,2) to (4,6): d = √(9+16) = √25 = 5.","sub":"","il":["Distance Between Two Points"]},{"term":"Midpoint","def":"The point halfway between two points: M = ((x₁+x₂)/2, (y₁+y₂)/2).","eg":"Midpoint of (2,4) and (6,10) = (4, 7).","sub":"","il":["Midpoint"]},{"term":"Circle — Standard Equation: (x−a)² + (y−b)² = r²","def":"A circle with centre (a, b) and radius r.","eg":"(x−3)² + (y+1)² = 25 has centre (3, −1) and radius 5.","sub":""},{"term":"Circle — General Equation: x² + y² + 2gx + 2fy + c = 0","def":"Rearrange by completing the square to find centre (−g, −f) and radius √(g²+f²−c).","eg":"x²+y²−4x+6y+9=0 → (x−2)²+(y+3)²=4. Centre (2,−3), radius 2.","sub":""},{"term":"Tangent to a Circle","def":"A straight line that touches the circle at exactly one point. At the point of tangency, the tangent is perpendicular to the radius.","eg":"Find the tangent to (x−2)²+(y−3)²=25 at (5,7): radius gradient=(7−3)/(5−2)=4/3, tangent gradient=−3/4.","sub":"","il":["Tangent to a Circle"]},{"term":"Chord","def":"A straight line segment with both endpoints on a circle.","eg":"A diameter is the longest chord, passing through the centre.","sub":""},{"term":"Asymptote","def":"A line that a curve approaches but never quite reaches. At A Level, these are usually horizontal or vertical.","eg":"For y = 1/x: vertical asymptote x=0, horizontal asymptote y=0.","sub":"","il":["Asymptote"]},{"term":"Parametric Equations","def":"Defining x and y separately in terms of a third variable (the parameter), usually t or θ.","eg":"x = t², y = 2t. To find the Cartesian equation: t = y/2, so x = (y/2)² → y² = 4x.","sub":"","il":["Parametric Equations"]},{"term":"Cartesian Equation","def":"An equation directly relating x and y, without a parameter.","eg":"y = x² is a Cartesian equation.","sub":"","il":["Cartesian Equation"]}]},{"n":"3","topic":"Sequences & Series","cluster":"seq","items":[{"term":"Sequence","def":"An ordered list of numbers following a pattern. Each number in the list is called a term.","eg":"1, 4, 9, 16, 25, … (square numbers).","sub":""},{"term":"Series","def":"The sum of the terms of a sequence. Contrast: a sequence is a list; a series is a sum.","eg":"1 + 4 + 9 + 16 + 25 is a series (the sum of the first 5 square numbers = 55).","sub":"","note":"'Sequence' and 'series' are NOT interchangeable — sequence = list, series = sum."},{"term":"Term (uₙ or aₙ)","def":"The nth term of a sequence. uₙ or aₙ denotes the term at position n.","eg":"For uₙ = 2n+1: u₁=3, u₂=5, u₃=7, …","sub":""},{"term":"Arithmetic Sequence / Arithmetic Progression (AP)","def":"A sequence where each term differs from the previous by a fixed amount called the common difference d. nth term: uₙ = a + (n−1)d, where a is the first term.","eg":"3, 7, 11, 15, … has a=3, d=4. u₁₀ = 3+9×4 = 39.","sub":"","il":["Arithmetic Sequence","Arithmetic Progression"]},{"term":"Common Difference (d)","def":"The constant value added to each term to get the next in an arithmetic sequence.","eg":"In 10, 7, 4, 1, … the common difference is d = −3.","sub":"","il":["Common Difference"]},{"term":"Sum of Arithmetic Series (Sₙ)","def":"Sₙ = n/2 × (2a + (n−1)d) = n/2 × (a + l), where l is the last term. Both forms are equivalent.","eg":"Sum of 1+2+3+…+100 = 100/2 × (1+100) = 5050.","sub":"","note":"Both formulae are on the formula sheet.","il":["Sum of Arithmetic Series"]},{"term":"Geometric Sequence / Geometric Progression (GP)","def":"A sequence where each term is obtained by multiplying the previous term by a fixed number called the common ratio r. nth term: uₙ = arⁿ⁻¹.","eg":"2, 6, 18, 54, … has a=2, r=3. u₅ = 2×3⁴ = 162.","sub":"","il":["Geometric Sequence","Geometric Progression"]},{"term":"Common Ratio (r)","def":"The constant multiplier between consecutive terms of a geometric sequence. r = uₙ₊₁ / uₙ.","eg":"In 100, 50, 25, 12.5, … the common ratio is r = 0.5.","sub":"","il":["Common Ratio"]},{"term":"Sum of Geometric Series (Sₙ)","def":"Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r) for r ≠ 1. Alternatively Sₙ = a(rⁿ−1)/(r−1). Both are equivalent — use whichever avoids negative denominators.","eg":"Sum of 2+6+18+…+2×3⁴ = 2(1−3⁵)/(1−3) = 2(−242)/(−2) = 242.","sub":"","il":["Sum of Geometric Series"]},{"term":"Sum to Infinity (S∞)","def":"For a geometric series with |r| < 1, as n→∞ the sum converges to S∞ = a/(1−r).","eg":"1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + … S∞ = 1/(1−0.5) = 2.","sub":"","note":"This only exists when |r| < 1. If |r| ≥ 1, the series diverges.","il":["Sum to Infinity"]},{"term":"Convergent Series","def":"A series whose partial sums approach a finite limit as n → ∞.","eg":"The geometric series with r=1/2 converges to S∞=2.","sub":"","il":["Convergent Series"]},{"term":"Divergent Series","def":"A series whose partial sums do not approach a finite limit.","eg":"1+2+4+8+… diverges since r=2>1.","sub":"","il":["Divergent Series"]},{"term":"Recurrence Relation","def":"A formula that expresses each term using one or more preceding terms.","eg":"uₙ₊₁ = 2uₙ + 1, u₁ = 3 → u₂=7, u₃=15, …","sub":"","il":["Recurrence Relation"]},{"term":"Binomial Expansion","def":"Expanding (a+b)ⁿ for positive integer n using the binomial theorem: (a+b)ⁿ = Σ ⁿCᵣ aⁿ⁻ʳ bʳ for r=0 to n. For (1+x)ⁿ with any real n and |x|<1: (1+x)ⁿ = 1 + nx + n(n−1)x²/2! + …","eg":"(1+x)³ = 1 + 3x + 3x² + x³.","sub":"","note":"For fractional/negative n, the expansion is infinite and valid only for |x|<1.","il":["Binomial Expansion"]},{"term":"Pascal's Triangle","def":"A triangular array where each entry is the sum of the two entries above it. The nth row (starting from row 0) gives the binomial coefficients ⁿC₀, ⁿC₁, …, ⁿCₙ.","eg":"Row 4: 1, 4, 6, 4, 1 → coefficients for (a+b)⁴.","sub":"","il":["Pascal's Triangle"]}]},{"n":"4","topic":"Trigonometry","cluster":"trig","items":[{"term":"Sine (sin)","def":"In a right-angled triangle: sin θ = opposite / hypotenuse. More generally, defined as the y-coordinate on the unit circle.","eg":"sin 30° = 0.5, sin(π/2) = 1.","sub":"Core Definitions","il":["sin","Sine"]},{"term":"Cosine (cos)","def":"In a right-angled triangle: cos θ = adjacent / hypotenuse. The x-coordinate on the unit circle.","eg":"cos 60° = 0.5, cos 0 = 1.","sub":"Core Definitions","il":["cos","Cosine"]},{"term":"Tangent (tan)","def":"tan θ = sin θ / cos θ = opposite / adjacent. Undefined where cos θ = 0.","eg":"tan 45° = 1, tan 90° is undefined.","sub":"Core Definitions","il":["tan"]},{"term":"Cosecant (cosec or csc)","def":"cosec θ = 1/sin θ. The reciprocal of sine. Undefined where sin θ = 0.","eg":"cosec 30° = 1/0.5 = 2.","sub":"Core Definitions","note":"'cosec' is standard UK notation; 'csc' is used in some other countries — interchangeable.","il":["cosec or csc","Cosecant"]},{"term":"Secant (sec)","def":"sec θ = 1/cos θ. The reciprocal of cosine. Undefined where cos θ = 0.","eg":"sec 60° = 1/0.5 = 2.","sub":"Core Definitions","il":["sec","Secant"]},{"term":"Cotangent (cot)","def":"cot θ = 1/tan θ = cos θ/sin θ. The reciprocal of tangent. Undefined where tan θ = 0.","eg":"cot 45° = 1.","sub":"Core Definitions","il":["cot","Cotangent"]},{"term":"Radian","def":"A unit of angle measurement. One radian is the angle subtended at the centre of a circle by an arc equal in length to the radius. π radians = 180°, so 1 rad ≈ 57.3°.","eg":"Convert 60° to radians: 60 × π/180 = π/3.","sub":"Core Definitions","note":"Radians are preferred in calculus since derivative formulae are only clean in radians."},{"term":"Exact Trigonometric Values","def":"Values of sin, cos, tan at standard angles that should be known without a calculator: 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 90° and their radian equivalents.","eg":"sin 45° = cos 45° = 1/√2 = √2/2; tan 60° = √3.","sub":"Core Definitions","il":["Exact Trigonometric Values"]},{"term":"Pythagorean Identity: sin²θ + cos²θ ≡ 1","def":"The fundamental trigonometric identity, derived from Pythagoras' theorem on the unit circle.","eg":"If sin θ = 3/5, then cos²θ = 1 − 9/25 = 16/25, so cos θ = ±4/5.","sub":"Identities"},{"term":"Pythagorean Identity: 1 + tan²θ ≡ sec²θ","def":"Divide sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 throughout by cos²θ.","eg":"Used to eliminate tan²θ or sec²θ in integration.","sub":"Identities"},{"term":"Pythagorean Identity: 1 + cot²θ ≡ cosec²θ","def":"Divide sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 throughout by sin²θ.","eg":"Used in integration with cosec and cot.","sub":"Identities"},{"term":"Double Angle Formulae","def":"sin 2A ≡ 2 sin A cos A. cos 2A ≡ cos²A − sin²A ≡ 2cos²A − 1 ≡ 1 − 2sin²A. tan 2A ≡ 2tan A / (1−tan²A). All given on the formula sheet.","eg":"cos 2A = 1−2sin²A rearranges to sin²A = (1−cos2A)/2, extremely useful in integration.","sub":"Identities","il":["Double Angle Formulae"]},{"term":"Addition Formulae (Compound Angle Formulae)","def":"sin(A±B) ≡ sinA cosB ± cosA sinB. cos(A±B) ≡ cosA cosB ∓ sinA sinB. tan(A±B) ≡ (tanA ± tanB)/(1 ∓ tanA tanB). All on the formula sheet.","eg":"sin 75° = sin(45°+30°) = sin45°cos30° + cos45°sin30° = (√6+√2)/4.","sub":"Identities","il":["Addition Formulae"]},{"term":"R sin(x + α) Form (Harmonic Form)","def":"Any expression a sinx + b cosx can be written as R sin(x + α), where R = √(a²+b²) and tan α = b/a. Useful for finding maximum/minimum values and solving equations.","eg":"3sinx + 4cosx = 5sin(x + α) where α = arctan(4/3) ≈ 53.1°. Maximum value is R=5.","sub":"Identities","il":["Harmonic Form","R sin Form"]},{"term":"Period","def":"The horizontal length after which a periodic function repeats. sin and cos have period 2π (360°). tan has period π (180°).","eg":"y = sin(2x) has period 2π/2 = π.","sub":"Graphs & Properties","il":["Period"]},{"term":"Amplitude","def":"Half the total vertical range of a periodic function. For y = A sin(x), amplitude = |A|.","eg":"y = 3cos(x) has amplitude 3 (range from −3 to 3).","sub":"Graphs & Properties","note":"Tangent has no amplitude (it is unbounded).","il":["Amplitude"]},{"term":"CAST Diagram","def":"A memory aid for the signs of trig functions in each quadrant. All positive (1st quadrant), Sine positive (2nd), Tangent positive (3rd), Cosine positive (4th).","eg":"sin(150°) = sin(180°−30°) = sin30° = 0.5 (2nd quadrant, sine positive).","sub":"Graphs & Properties","il":["CAST Diagram"]},{"term":"Principal Value","def":"The unique value returned by an inverse trig function. arcsin has range [−π/2, π/2]; arccos has range [0, π]; arctan has range (−π/2, π/2).","eg":"arcsin(0.5) = π/6 (the principal value). The other solution in [0,2π] is 5π/6.","sub":"Graphs & Properties","il":["Principal Value"]},{"term":"Arcsin / Arccos / Arctan (Inverse Trig Functions)","def":"The inverse functions of sin, cos, tan respectively (on restricted domains). Also written sin⁻¹, cos⁻¹, tan⁻¹.","eg":"arctan(1) = π/4.","sub":"Graphs & Properties","note":"sin⁻¹ does NOT mean 1/sin. It means the inverse function of sin.","il":["Inverse Trig Functions","Arcsin","Arccos","Arctan"]},{"term":"Sine Rule","def":"a/sinA = b/sinB = c/sinC (or inverted). Used when you have a side and its opposite angle, plus one more piece of information.","eg":"In triangle ABC, a=7, A=40°, B=60°. Then b = 7sin60°/sin40° ≈ 9.42.","sub":"Sine & Cosine Rule","note":"Watch out for the ambiguous case when using the sine rule to find an angle.","il":["Sine Rule"]},{"term":"Cosine Rule","def":"a² = b² + c² − 2bc cosA (and rearranges to cos A = (b²+c²−a²)/2bc). Used when given two sides and included angle (SAS) or all three sides (SSS).","eg":"a²=5²+7²−2(5)(7)cos60°=25+49−35=39, so a=√39.","sub":"Sine & Cosine Rule","il":["Cosine Rule"]},{"term":"Area of a Triangle: ½ab sinC","def":"For a triangle with two sides a, b and included angle C: Area = ½ab sinC.","eg":"Sides 6 and 8 with included angle 30°: Area = ½×6×8×sin30° = 12.","sub":"Sine & Cosine Rule"}]},{"n":"5","topic":"Exponentials & Logarithms","cluster":"exp","items":[{"term":"Exponential Function","def":"A function of the form f(x) = aˣ where a > 0 and a ≠ 1. The variable is the exponent.","eg":"f(x) = 2ˣ: growth. f(x) = (0.5)ˣ: decay.","sub":"","note":"Do not confuse with power functions like x³ where the variable is the base.","il":["Exponential Function"]},{"term":"The Number e (Euler's Number)","def":"An irrational constant ≈ 2.71828… defined as the base of the natural exponential function. e is the unique number such that d/dx(eˣ) = eˣ. Also defined as the limit of (1+1/n)ⁿ as n→∞.","eg":"e¹ ≈ 2.718, e⁰ = 1, e⁻¹ ≈ 0.368.","sub":"","il":["Euler's Number","The Number e"]},{"term":"Natural Exponential Function: y = eˣ","def":"The exponential function with base e. The only function that is its own derivative: d/dx(eˣ) = eˣ. Domain: all real numbers. Range: y > 0. Passes through (0,1).","eg":"y = 3e²ˣ → dy/dx = 6e²ˣ (chain rule gives the factor 2).","sub":""},{"term":"Logarithm","def":"The inverse operation to exponentiation. logₐ(x) = y means aʸ = x. It answers the question: 'to what power must I raise a to get x?'","eg":"log₂(8) = 3 because 2³ = 8.","sub":"","il":["Logarithm"]},{"term":"Common Logarithm (log₁₀ or just log)","def":"Logarithm with base 10. log₁₀(x) is often written simply as log(x) at A Level (context-dependent).","eg":"log(100) = 2 because 10² = 100.","sub":"","note":"Always check context — 'log' without a base may mean log₁₀ or loge depending on the textbook.","il":["Common Logarithm"]},{"term":"Natural Logarithm (ln)","def":"Logarithm with base e. Written ln(x) or logₑ(x) — these are identical. ln and eˣ are inverses: ln(eˣ) = x and e^(ln x) = x.","eg":"ln(e³) = 3. ln(1) = 0. ln(e) = 1.","sub":"","il":["ln","Natural Logarithm"]},{"term":"Laws of Logarithms","def":"Three key rules: (1) log(AB) = log A + log B. (2) log(A/B) = log A − log B. (3) log(Aⁿ) = n log A. These hold for any base (as long as you're consistent).","eg":"ln(e²x³) = ln(e²) + ln(x³) = 2 + 3ln(x).","sub":"","il":["Laws of Logarithms"]},{"term":"Change of Base Formula","def":"logₐ(x) = log_b(x) / log_b(a) for any valid base b. Allows conversion between bases.","eg":"log₂(10) = ln(10)/ln(2) ≈ 3.322.","sub":"","note":"This formula is on the formula sheet.","il":["Change of Base Formula"]},{"term":"Exponential Growth and Decay","def":"Models of the form y = Aeᵏᵗ. If k > 0: growth. If k < 0: decay. Key property: rate of change is proportional to current value (dy/dt = ky).","eg":"Population P = 1000e^(0.02t). At t=0, P=1000. At t=10, P=1000e^0.2 ≈ 1221.","sub":"","il":["Exponential Growth and Decay"]},{"term":"Logarithmic Graphs","def":"y = ln(x) is the reflection of y = eˣ in the line y = x. Domain x>0, range all reals. Passes through (1,0).","eg":"y = ln(x+2) shifts the graph left by 2 (domain x > −2).","sub":"","il":["Logarithmic Graphs"]}]},{"n":"6","topic":"Differentiation","cluster":"diff","items":[{"term":"Derivative / Differentiation","def":"The derivative of y with respect to x measures the instantaneous rate of change of y as x changes. Written dy/dx, y', or f'(x). The process of finding it is called differentiation.","eg":"If y = x³, then dy/dx = 3x².","sub":"Core Concepts","note":"'Derivative', 'differential' (loosely), 'gradient function', and 'rate of change' all refer to the same concept.","il":["Differentiation"]},{"term":"First Principles (Definition of Derivative)","def":"f'(x) = lim(h→0) [(f(x+h) − f(x)) / h]. The derivative as the limit of the gradient of a chord as the gap shrinks to zero.","eg":"f(x)=x²: f'(x) = lim[(x+h)²−x²]/h = lim[2xh+h²]/h = lim[2x+h] = 2x.","sub":"Core Concepts","il":["First Principles"]},{"term":"Gradient Function","def":"Another name for the derivative dy/dx. It gives the gradient of the curve at any point x.","eg":"For y=x³, the gradient function is 3x². At x=2, gradient=12.","sub":"Core Concepts","note":"Interchangeable with 'derivative' in most contexts.","il":["Gradient Function"]},{"term":"Tangent (to a curve)","def":"A straight line that touches the curve at exactly one point (locally) and has the same gradient as the curve at that point.","eg":"y = x² at x=3: gradient=6, point=(3,9). Tangent: y−9=6(x−3) → y=6x−9.","sub":"Core Concepts","il":["to a curve"]},{"term":"Normal (to a curve)","def":"A line perpendicular to the tangent at a given point on the curve. If the tangent has gradient m, the normal has gradient −1/m.","eg":"At (3,9) on y=x², tangent gradient=6, so normal gradient=−1/6. Normal: y−9=−(1/6)(x−3).","sub":"Core Concepts","il":["to a curve"]},{"term":"Power Rule: d/dx(xⁿ) = nxⁿ⁻¹","def":"Multiply by the power, then reduce the power by 1. Works for all real n (including fractions and negatives).","eg":"d/dx(x⁵) = 5x⁴. d/dx(x^(1/2)) = (1/2)x^(−1/2).","sub":"Rules of Differentiation"},{"term":"Chain Rule: dy/dx = dy/du × du/dx","def":"Used to differentiate a function of a function (composite function). If y = f(g(x)), then dy/dx = f'(g(x)) × g'(x).","eg":"y = (3x+1)⁵: let u=3x+1, y=u⁵. dy/dx = 5u⁴ × 3 = 15(3x+1)⁴.","sub":"Rules of Differentiation"},{"term":"Product Rule: d/dx(uv) = u(dv/dx) + v(du/dx)","def":"Used when differentiating the product of two functions.","eg":"y = x²sin(x): dy/dx = x²cos(x) + 2xsin(x).","sub":"Rules of Differentiation"},{"term":"Quotient Rule: d/dx(u/v) = (v(du/dx) − u(dv/dx)) / v²","def":"Used when differentiating one function divided by another.","eg":"y = sin(x)/x²: dy/dx = (x²cos(x) − 2xsin(x)) / x⁴.","sub":"Rules of Differentiation","note":"The quotient rule is on the formula sheet. Alternatively, rewrite as u×v⁻¹ and use the product rule."},{"term":"Implicit Differentiation","def":"Differentiating an equation where y is not isolated. Differentiate both sides with respect to x, applying the chain rule to y terms (d/dx[f(y)] = f'(y) × dy/dx), then rearrange for dy/dx.","eg":"x²+y²=25 → 2x+2y(dy/dx)=0 → dy/dx = −x/y.","sub":"Rules of Differentiation","il":["Implicit Differentiation"]},{"term":"Derivatives of Standard Functions","def":"Key results to know: d/dx(eˣ)=eˣ, d/dx(eᵏˣ)=keᵏˣ, d/dx(ln x)=1/x, d/dx(sin x)=cos x, d/dx(cos x)=−sin x, d/dx(tan x)=sec²x, d/dx(sin kx)=k cos kx, d/dx(cos kx)=−k sin kx. Also: d/dx(aˣ)=aˣ ln a.","eg":"d/dx(sin(3x)) = 3cos(3x) by chain rule.","sub":"Key Derivatives","note":"All must be in radians for the trig derivatives to be valid.","il":["Derivatives of Standard Functions"]},{"term":"Derivatives of Inverse Trig Functions","def":"d/dx(arcsin x) = 1/√(1−x²), d/dx(arccos x) = −1/√(1−x²), d/dx(arctan x) = 1/(1+x²).","eg":"d/dx(arctan(2x)) = 2/(1+4x²) by chain rule.","sub":"Key Derivatives","il":["Derivatives of Inverse Trig Functions"]},{"term":"Stationary Point / Turning Point","def":"A point where dy/dx = 0 (the gradient is zero). The curve is neither increasing nor decreasing at this point.","eg":"y=x³−3x: dy/dx=3x²−3=0 → x=±1. Stationary points at (1,−2) and (−1,2).","sub":"Applications","note":"'Stationary point' and 'turning point' are sometimes used interchangeably, but technically a stationary point includes points of inflection where the curve doesn't turn.","il":["Stationary Point","Turning Point"]},{"term":"Local Maximum","def":"A stationary point where the function value is greater than nearby points. Identified by dy/dx=0 and d²y/dx²<0.","eg":"y=−x²+4: dy/dx=−2x=0→x=0. d²y/dx²=−2<0, so (0,4) is a local max.","sub":"Applications","il":["Local Maximum"]},{"term":"Local Minimum","def":"A stationary point where the function value is less than nearby points. Identified by dy/dx=0 and d²y/dx²>0.","eg":"y=x²−4x: dy/dx=2x−4=0→x=2. d²y/dx²=2>0, so (2,−4) is a local min.","sub":"Applications","il":["Local Minimum"]},{"term":"Point of Inflection","def":"A point where the curve changes concavity (from concave up to concave down or vice versa). At a stationary point of inflection, dy/dx=0 AND d²y/dx²=0 (but the gradient doesn't change sign).","eg":"y=x³ at x=0: dy/dx=0 and d²y/dx²=0, and gradient doesn't change sign → stationary point of inflection.","sub":"Applications","il":["Point of Inflection"]},{"term":"Second Derivative (d²y/dx²)","def":"The derivative of the derivative. Measures the rate of change of gradient; indicates concavity. Also written f''(x) or y''.","eg":"y=x⁴: dy/dx=4x³, d²y/dx²=12x².","sub":"Applications","il":["Second Derivative"]},{"term":"Increasing / Decreasing Function","def":"f is increasing on an interval if dy/dx > 0 throughout. Decreasing if dy/dx < 0.","eg":"y=x²: dy/dx=2x. Decreasing for x<0, increasing for x>0.","sub":"Applications","il":["Increasing","Decreasing Function"]},{"term":"Optimisation","def":"Using differentiation to find the maximum or minimum value of a quantity, typically in a real-world context. Set dy/dx=0 and verify using second derivative or sign analysis.","eg":"Maximise area A=xy subject to 2x+2y=20: substitute y=10−x, A=x(10−x)=10x−x².","sub":"Applications","il":["Optimisation"]},{"term":"dA/dx=10−2x=0→x=5.","def":"Connected Rates of Change Using the chain rule to relate rates of change of different quantities: dy/dt = dy/dx × dx/dt.","eg":"If A=πr² and dr/dt=2, then dA/dt = 2πr × 2 = 4πr.","sub":"Applications"}]},{"n":"7","topic":"Integration","cluster":"integ","items":[{"term":"Integration","def":"The reverse process of differentiation. Also used to find areas under curves. The integral of f(x) with respect to x is written ∫ f(x) dx.","eg":"∫ 3x² dx = x³ + C.","sub":"Core Concepts","il":["Integration"]},{"term":"Indefinite Integral","def":"An integral without limits. The result includes a constant of integration C (since differentiating a constant gives 0).","eg":"∫ 2x dx = x² + C.","sub":"Core Concepts","note":"Always include '+ C' for indefinite integrals — forgetting it costs marks.","il":["Indefinite Integral"]},{"term":"Definite Integral","def":"An integral with upper and lower limits. Gives a numerical value representing the signed area between the curve and x-axis. ∫ₐᵇ f(x)dx = [F(x)]ₐᵇ = F(b)−F(a).","eg":"∫₁³ 2x dx = [x²]₁³ = 9−1 = 8.","sub":"Core Concepts","il":["Definite Integral"]},{"term":"Constant of Integration (C)","def":"An arbitrary constant added to every indefinite integral, accounting for the fact that many functions have the same derivative.","eg":"∫ cos x dx = sin x + C. All of sin x + 1, sin x + 7, sin x − π have derivative cos x.","sub":"Core Concepts","il":["Constant of Integration"]},{"term":"Anti-derivative / Primitive","def":"Another name for the indefinite integral. F(x) is an anti-derivative of f(x) if F'(x) = f(x). These terms are interchangeable.","eg":"x³ is an anti-derivative of 3x².","sub":"Core Concepts","il":["Anti-derivative","Primitive"]},{"term":"Fundamental Theorem of Calculus","def":"Links differentiation and integration: if F'(x)=f(x), then ∫ₐᵇ f(x)dx = F(b)−F(a). It guarantees that integration and differentiation are inverse operations.","eg":"∫₀² x² dx = [x³/3]₀² = 8/3.","sub":"Core Concepts","il":["Fundamental Theorem of Calculus"]},{"term":"Power Rule for Integration: ∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n+1) + C (n ≠ −1)","def":"Add 1 to the power, then divide by the new power. Valid for all n except n=−1.","eg":"∫ x⁴ dx = x⁵/5 + C. ∫ x^(1/2) dx = (2/3)x^(3/2) + C.","sub":"Standard Integrals"},{"term":"∫ 1/x dx = ln|x| + C","def":"The special case excluded from the power rule (n=−1). The absolute value is required since ln is only defined for positive arguments.","eg":"∫ 3/x dx = 3 ln|x| + C.","sub":"Standard Integrals"},{"term":"Standard Trig Integrals","def":"∫ sin x dx = −cos x + C. ∫ cos x dx = sin x + C. ∫ sec²x dx = tan x + C. ∫ cosec²x dx = −cot x + C. ∫ sec x tan x dx = sec x + C. ∫ cosec x cot x dx = −cosec x + C.","eg":"∫ cos(3x) dx = (1/3)sin(3x) + C.","sub":"Standard Integrals","il":["Standard Trig Integrals"]},{"term":"Standard Exponential Integrals","def":"∫ eˣ dx = eˣ + C. ∫ eᵏˣ dx = (1/k)eᵏˣ + C. ∫ aˣ dx = aˣ/ln(a) + C.","eg":"∫ e^(2x) dx = (1/2)e^(2x) + C.","sub":"Standard Integrals","il":["Standard Exponential Integrals"]},{"term":"Integration by Substitution","def":"Change the variable to simplify the integral. Let u = g(x), then du = g'(x)dx. Replace all x terms and dx with u terms and du. Analogue of the chain rule for integration.","eg":"∫ 2x(x²+1)⁴ dx. Let u=x²+1, du=2x dx. → ∫ u⁴ du = u⁵/5 + C = (x²+1)⁵/5 + C.","sub":"Techniques","il":["Integration by Substitution"]},{"term":"Integration by Parts: ∫ u dv = uv − ∫ v du","def":"Used to integrate products of functions. Choose u and dv using LIATE order (Logarithm, Inverse trig, Algebraic, Trig, Exponential) — pick u from left of this list.","eg":"∫ x eˣ dx: u=x, dv=eˣdx → du=dx, v=eˣ. = xeˣ − ∫eˣdx = xeˣ − eˣ + C = eˣ(x−1)+C.","sub":"Techniques","note":"This formula is on the formula sheet."},{"term":"Integration using Partial Fractions","def":"Split a rational function into partial fractions first, then integrate term by term.","eg":"∫ 1/((x+1)(x+2)) dx = ∫ [1/(x+1) − 1/(x+2)] dx = ln|x+1| − ln|x+2| + C.","sub":"Techniques","il":["Integration using Partial Fractions"]},{"term":"Integration using Trig Identities","def":"Rewrite the integrand using identities (especially double angle formulae) to make it integrable.","eg":"∫ sin²x dx = ∫ (1−cos2x)/2 dx = x/2 − sin(2x)/4 + C.","sub":"Techniques","il":["Integration using Trig Identities"]},{"term":"Trapezium Rule","def":"A numerical method to approximate ∫ₐᵇ f(x)dx by dividing the area into trapezoids: ≈ (h/2)[y₀ + 2(y₁+y₂+…+yₙ₋₁) + yₙ] where h=(b−a)/n.","eg":"Approximating ∫₀¹ eˣ dx with 4 strips: h=0.25, apply formula.","sub":"Techniques","note":"On the formula sheet. Gives an overestimate for concave-up (convex) curves, underestimate for concave-down.","il":["Trapezium Rule"]},{"term":"Area Under a Curve","def":"∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx gives the signed area between y=f(x) and the x-axis from x=a to x=b. Areas below the x-axis give negative values — take the absolute value for geometric area.","eg":"Area under y=x² from 0 to 3: ∫₀³ x² dx = [x³/3]₀³ = 9.","sub":"Applications of Integration","il":["Area Under a Curve"]},{"term":"Area Between Two Curves","def":"∫ₐᵇ [f(x) − g(x)] dx where f(x) ≥ g(x) throughout [a,b]. Find intersection points first to determine limits.","eg":"Area between y=x² and y=x: intersect at 0 and 1. ∫₀¹ (x−x²)dx = [x²/2−x³/3]₀¹ = 1/6.","sub":"Applications of Integration","il":["Area Between Two Curves"]},{"term":"Volume of Revolution (about x-axis)","def":"Rotating y=f(x) about the x-axis from x=a to x=b gives volume V = π∫ₐᵇ y² dx.","eg":"Rotating y=√x about x-axis from 0 to 4: V=π∫₀⁴ x dx = π[x²/2]₀⁴ = 8π.","sub":"Applications of Integration","note":"On the formula sheet.","il":["about x-axis","Volume of Revolution"]}]},{"n":"8","topic":"Numerical Methods","cluster":"num","items":[{"term":"Numerical Methods","def":"Techniques for finding approximate solutions to equations or integrals that cannot be solved analytically (exactly).","eg":"Solving x³+x−5=0 has no simple algebraic solution — use numerical methods.","sub":"","il":["Numerical Methods"]},{"term":"Root-finding","def":"The process of finding the value(s) of x for which f(x)=0.","eg":"Find where x³−x−1=0.","sub":"","il":["Root-finding"]},{"term":"Change of Sign Method","def":"If f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs and f is continuous on [a,b], then there is at least one root in (a,b).","eg":"f(1)=-1<0, f(2)=5>0 → root of f(x)=x³−x−1 lies in (1,2).","sub":"","note":"This fails if there's a discontinuity, or if there are two roots in the interval that cancel.","il":["Change of Sign Method"]},{"term":"Decimal Search / Interval Bisection","def":"Repeatedly halve the interval (or try values between) to narrow down the root's location.","eg":"Root in (1,2): try x=1.5. f(1.5)=0.875>0 → root in (1,1.5). Try x=1.25. Continue…","sub":"","il":["Decimal Search","Interval Bisection"]},{"term":"Newton-Raphson Method","def":"An iterative formula: xₙ₊₁ = xₙ − f(xₙ)/f'(xₙ). Starting from an initial estimate x₀, each iteration gives a better approximation to the root.","eg":"f(x)=x²−2, f'(x)=2x. x₀=1.5: x₁=1.5−(2.25−2)/3=1.5−0.083=1.417. (√2≈1.414).","sub":"","note":"On the formula sheet. Can fail if x₀ is near a turning point (f'(xₙ)≈0).","il":["Newton-Raphson Method"]},{"term":"Fixed-Point Iteration","def":"Rearrange f(x)=0 into the form x=g(x), then iterate xₙ₊₁ = g(xₙ). Converges if |g'(x)| < 1 near the root.","eg":"x³=x+1 → x=∛(x+1). x₀=1.3: x₁=∛2.3≈1.320, x₂=∛2.320≈1.323, …","sub":"","note":"Convergence is not guaranteed — always verify by checking |g'(x)|<1.","il":["Fixed-Point Iteration"]},{"term":"Staircase Diagram / Cobweb Diagram","def":"Graphical representations of iterative convergence or divergence. Staircase: monotonic convergence. Cobweb: oscillating convergence or divergence.","eg":"Plot y=x and y=g(x); trace the iteration to see whether it spirals in (cobweb) or steps in","sub":"","il":["Staircase Diagram","Cobweb Diagram"]},{"term":"(staircase).","def":"Convergence An iterative sequence converges if it gets closer and closer to a fixed limit.","eg":"xₙ=1+1/n → 1 as n→∞.","sub":"","il":["staircase"]},{"term":"Divergence (Iterative)","def":"An iterative process diverges if the values do not approach a limit (they grow or oscillate without settling).","eg":"xₙ₊₁ = 2xₙ starting from x₀=1 diverges: 1, 2, 4, 8, …","sub":"","il":["Iterative","Divergence"]}]},{"n":"9","topic":"Proof","cluster":"proof","items":[{"term":"Mathematical Proof","def":"A rigorous logical argument that establishes the truth of a statement beyond doubt, proceeding from known assumptions through logical steps to a conclusion.","eg":"Proving that the sum of two odd numbers is always even.","sub":"","il":["Mathematical Proof"]},{"term":"Proof by Deduction","def":"The most common form. Start from known facts or given assumptions and proceed step-by-step using logical deductions to reach the conclusion.","eg":"Prove n²−6n+10 > 0 for all n: n²−6n+10=(n−3)²+1 ≥ 1 > 0. ✓","sub":"","il":["Proof by Deduction"]},{"term":"Proof by Exhaustion","def":"Check all possible cases individually. Only feasible when the number of cases is finite and small.","eg":"Prove that for prime p with 3<p<25, (p−1)(p+1) is a multiple of 12. Check p=5,7,11,13,17,19,23 each separately.","sub":"","il":["Proof by Exhaustion"]},{"term":"Proof by Contradiction","def":"Assume the opposite of what you want to prove. Show this assumption leads to a contradiction (an impossibility), which means the original statement must be true.","eg":"Prove √2 is irrational: assume √2=p/q in lowest terms. Then 2q²=p², so p is even… eventually both p and q are even, contradicting lowest terms. Contradiction. ✓","sub":"","il":["Proof by Contradiction"]},{"term":"Disproof by Counter-example","def":"To disprove a universal statement ('P is true for all n'), you only need to find ONE specific case where it fails.","eg":"Disprove 'n²+n+41 is prime for all n': try n=40 → 40²+40+41=1681=41². Not prime. ✓","sub":"","note":"A single counter-example is sufficient to disprove a 'for all' claim.","il":["Disproof by Counter-example"]},{"term":"Conjecture","def":"A statement believed to be true but not yet proven.","eg":"Goldbach's conjecture: every even integer > 2 is the sum of two primes. Unproven.","sub":"","il":["Conjecture"]},{"term":"Necessary Condition","def":"B is necessary for A means: if A is true, then B must be true (A ⇒ B). B being true doesn't guarantee A.","eg":"Being divisible by 4 is necessary for being divisible by 8. (Divisible by 8 ⇒ divisible by 4.)","sub":"","il":["Necessary Condition"]},{"term":"Sufficient Condition","def":"A is sufficient for B means: A being true guarantees B is true (A ⇒ B). B might be true for other reasons too.","eg":"Being divisible by 8 is sufficient for being divisible by 4.","sub":"","il":["Sufficient Condition"]},{"term":"Necessary and Sufficient (iff)","def":"A is necessary AND sufficient for B means A ⟺ B. A is true exactly when B is true.","eg":"n is even ⟺ n² is even. Each condition is necessary and sufficient for the other.","sub":"","il":["Necessary and Sufficient"]}]},{"n":"10","topic":"Vectors","cluster":"vectors","items":[{"term":"Scalar","def":"A quantity with magnitude (size) only, no direction.","eg":"Temperature, speed, mass, and time are scalars.","sub":""},{"term":"Vector","def":"A quantity with both magnitude and direction. Written in bold (a), underlined (a), or as a column vector.","eg":"Velocity, displacement, force, and acceleration are vectors.","sub":"","note":"Contrast with scalars: speed is scalar (50 mph), velocity is vector (50 mph north)."},{"term":"Position Vector","def":"The vector from the origin O to a point P, written as OP⃗ or p. If P = (3, 4, 5), then p = 3i + 4j + 5k.","eg":"Point A(2,3): position vector a = 2i + 3j.","sub":"","il":["Position Vector"]},{"term":"Column Vector Notation","def":"A vector written as a vertical array of components: (a, b) in 2D or (a, b, c) in 3D.","eg":"(3, −2) represents 3 units in x and −2 in y.","sub":"","il":["Column Vector Notation"]},{"term":"Unit Vectors i, j, k","def":"i = (1,0,0), j = (0,1,0), k = (0,0,1). The standard basis vectors in 3D. Any vector can be written as a combination: a = xi + yj + zk.","eg":"2i − 3j + k represents the vector (2, −3, 1).","sub":""},{"term":"Magnitude (Modulus) of a Vector","def":"|a| = √(x²+y²) in 2D; √(x²+y²+z²) in 3D. The length (size) of the vector.","eg":"|3i + 4j| = √(9+16) = 5.","sub":"","note":"'Magnitude' and 'modulus' are interchangeable for vectors.","il":["Modulus","Magnitude  of a Vector"]},{"term":"Unit Vector (in direction of a)","def":"A vector of magnitude 1 in the direction of a: â = a/|a|.","eg":"a=3i+4j, |a|=5. Unit vector = (3i+4j)/5 = 0.6i+0.8j.","sub":"","il":["in direction of a","Unit Vector"]},{"term":"Vector Addition","def":"Add corresponding components: (a₁,a₂) + (b₁,b₂) = (a₁+b₁, a₂+b₂). Geometrically, place vectors end to end.","eg":"(2,3) + (−1,5) = (1,8).","sub":"","il":["Vector Addition"]},{"term":"Scalar Multiplication","def":"Multiply each component by the scalar: k(a₁,a₂) = (ka₁,ka₂). Scales the vector's length; reverses direction if k<0.","eg":"3(2,−1) = (6,−3).","sub":"","il":["Scalar Multiplication"]},{"term":"Parallel Vectors","def":"Two vectors are parallel if one is a scalar multiple of the other: b = ka for some scalar k.","eg":"(2,4) and (1,2) are parallel since (2,4) = 2(1,2).","sub":"","il":["Parallel Vectors"]},{"term":"Scalar (Dot) Product","def":"a·b = |a||b|cosθ = a₁b₁ + a₂b₂ + a₃b₃. A scalar result. If a·b = 0 and neither vector is zero, the vectors are perpendicular.","eg":"(2,3)·(1,−1) = 2(1)+3(−1) = −1. cos θ = −1/(√13 × √2).","sub":"","il":["Scalar  Product"]},{"term":"Perpendicular Vectors","def":"Two non-zero vectors are perpendicular if and only if their dot product is zero: a·b = 0.","eg":"Is (3,−2)·(2,3) = 0? 6+(−6)=0. Yes, perpendicular.","sub":"","il":["Perpendicular Vectors"]},{"term":"Vector Equation of a Line","def":"r = a + λb, where a is a position vector on the line, b is the direction vector, and λ is a scalar parameter.","eg":"Line through (1,2,3) in direction (4,0,−1): r = (1,2,3) + λ(4,0,−1).","sub":"","il":["Vector Equation of a Line"]},{"term":"Direction Vector","def":"A vector parallel to a given line, indicating its direction. Any scalar multiple is also a valid direction vector.","eg":"Direction vector of y=2x+5 is (1,2) (gradient 2, so for every 1 across, go 2 up).","sub":"","il":["Direction Vector"]},{"term":"Collinear Points","def":"Three or more points are collinear if they all lie on the same straight line. Vectors AB⃗ and AC⃗ are parallel (one is a multiple of the other) and share point A.","eg":"A(1,2), B(3,6), C(5,10): AB=(2,4), AC=(4,8)=2(2,4). Parallel and share A → collinear.","sub":"","il":["Collinear Points"]},{"term":"Displacement Vector (AB⃗)","def":"The vector from point A to point B: AB⃗ = b − a (position vector of B minus position vector of A).","eg":"A=(1,3), B=(4,7): AB⃗ = (4−1, 7−3) = (3,4).","sub":"","il":["Displacement Vector"]}]}];
